    "theorem": "Let V_N be nested finite-dimensional subspaces with dense union. Suppose a truncated Hermitian form q_(N,T) satisfies |q(v)-q_(N,T)(v)| <= B_N(T)||v||^2 on V_N and lambda_min(q_(N,T)) >= -delta_(N,T). If a diagonal schedule T_N has delta_(N,T_N)+B_N(T_N) tending to zero, then q is nonnegative. For the explicit Corollary 3.3 upper bound U_N(T)=2(2N+1)rho/pi^2 times [log(T)/(T-rho N)+(rho N)^(-1)log(T/(T-rho N))], any schedule with T_N/(N log T_N) tending to infinity makes U_N(T_N) tend to zero. T_N=N^2 works, whereas T_N=C N and T_N=C N log N do not make this certified upper bound vanish.",
    "proof": "The perturbation inequality gives q(v)>=-[delta_(N,T)+B_N(T)]||v||^2 on each V_N. Apply the TICKET-173 dense-core argument along the diagonal schedule. In the explicit bound, the hypothesis makes rho N/T_N tend to zero, so T_N-rho N is asymptotic to T_N. The first term is O(N log(T_N)/T_N), and the second is O(N/T_N). With T_N=N^2 both vanish. With T_N=C N the first term grows like log N; with T_N=C N log N it approaches a positive constant.",
